<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ulrich von Jungingen</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1360">1360</a>–<a href="/years/1410">1410</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ulrich von Jungingen was Grand Master of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/teutonic-order">Teutonic Order</a> from 1407 to 1410. Taking a hard line towards <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lithuania">Lithuania</a> over <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samogitia">Samogitia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dobrzyn-land">Dobrzyń Land</a>, he went to war with them in 1409. He was killed on 15 July 1410 at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-grunwald">Battle of Grunwald</a>, together with much of the Order's leadership, a defeat from which the Order's military power never recovered.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ulrich-von-jungingen.jpg" alt="Ulrich von Jungingen" width="320" height="372" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: MostEpic · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Arms_of_the_house_of_Jungingen_as_Grand_Master_of_the_Teutonic_Order.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Ulrich came from the Swabian noble house of Jungingen and was probably born at Hohenfels Castle near Stockach. As a younger son excluded from the succession, he and his elder brother Konrad von Jungingen took the vows of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/teutonic-order">Teutonic Knights</a> and moved to the Order's state in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a>. Ulrich lived at Schlochau and was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/komtur">Komtur</a> of Balga from 1396 to 1404, and his career profited from Konrad's patronage after Konrad was elected Grand Master in 1393. He distinguished himself in the negotiations over Gotland with Queen <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/margaret-i-of-denmark">Margaret I of Denmark</a>, after the Knights had expelled the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/victual-brothers">Victual Brothers</a> from the island in 1398, and on diplomatic missions to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lithuania">Lithuania</a> connected with the Treaty of Salynas.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">In office</h2><p>In 1404 Ulrich became the Order's Marshal and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/komtur">Komtur</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/konigsberg">Königsberg</a>, and he dealt with several Samogitian uprisings by strict suppression and by bribing local nobles. When Konrad died suddenly in 1407, Ulrich was chosen Grand Master on 26 June. His diplomatic ability was limited compared with his brother's. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samogitia">Samogitia</a> stayed tense, since Grand Duke Vytautas of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lithuania">Lithuania</a> meant to use the unrest to win back the ceded lands, and a dispute with King Jagiełło of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> over <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dobrzyn-land">Dobrzyń Land</a> and the pawned <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neumark">Neumark</a> was growing.</p><p>Despite the threat of a war on two fronts, Ulrich prepared a pre-emptive strike, allied with King Sigismund of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a>, hired <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mercenary">mercenaries</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-roman-empire">Holy Roman Empire</a> and declared war on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> on 6 August 1409. Sigismund sent no help, but the Order's forces first campaigned successfully in Dobrzyń and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kuyavia">Kuyavia</a>.</p><p>On 2 July 1410 Ulrich led his army out of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malbork-castle">Malbork Castle</a>. The armies met on 15 July between Grunwald and Stębark. According to Jan Długosz, he had two swords sent to Jagiełło, a gesture seen as a provocation that set off the Polish-Lithuanian attack. Ulrich led his remaining regiments in person, nearly reached the king, but lost oversight of the battle. The Prussian forces of the Lizard Union broke away, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lithuanians">Lithuanians</a> attacked from the rear, and he was killed. Jagiełło had his body taken to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malbork">Malbork</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Following Długosz, later generations have called Ulrich hot-blooded and arrogant. Jan Matejko's painting Battle of Grunwald and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henryk-sienkiewicz">Henryk Sienkiewicz</a>'s 1900 novel The Knights of the Cross present him so, while nineteenth-century German historiography portrayed him as a man of chivalric virtues who fell to his enemies' cunning. His successor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heinrich-von-plauen">Heinrich von Plauen</a> had a chapel built on the battlefield in 1413. In 1901 the German authorities in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-prussia">East Prussia</a> set up a boulder, the Jungingenstein, in his memory; a Polish stone nearby marks the place of his death, and the area hosts an annual reenactment.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Ulrich von Jungingen” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Order's military reputation collapsed at the Battle of Grunwald (Tannenberg) on 15 July 1410, when a Polish-Lithuanian army under Władysław II Jagiełło and Vytautas killed Grand Master <b>Ulrich von Jungingen</b> and much of the Order's leadership.</blockquote><a href="/empire/state-of-the-teutonic-order">State of the Teutonic Order · Military</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Hohenfels, in what is now <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>. Died in Stębark, in what is now <a href="/countries/poland">Poland</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=47.89,9.11&amp;year=1392" aria-label="Hohenfels on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Ulrich von Jungingen"><image href="/maps/globes/g000n60.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5520 5992h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5520 5992h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=47.89,9.11&amp;year=1392">What was here in 1392? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
